# Valgrind suppressions for the C++ binding's memory check.
#
# Rust's `Option<f64>` has no niche: every bit pattern is a valid `f64`, so a
# `None` writes only the discriminant and leaves the payload bytes unwritten.
# `compute_intervals` receives `delta1`/`delta2` as `Option<f64>`, and they are
# `None` whenever the fit ran without hat-matrix leverage (the default
# `surface_mode = "interpolation"`). At opt-level 3 LLVM speculates the payload
# comparison in the degrees-of-freedom check ahead of the discriminant test, so
# Memcheck sees a conditional jump on those never-written bytes. The comparison
# result is discarded when the discriminant is `None`, so this is a false
# positive rather than a defect.
#
# Scoped to `Memcheck:Cond` in this one function: invalid reads/writes, bad
# frees, and leaks anywhere still fail the check.
{
   rust-option-none-payload-compute-intervals
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:*compute_intervals*
}
